window.docContents[27].resolve({"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Weighted-moment-bridge":{"contents":"For a scalar field , measure , and spatial weight , defineThe spatial type and measure are arbitrary. Euclidean geometry enters only\nthrough a model-specific choice of weight. Mathlib's Bochner integral is\ntotalized, so the weighted spatial moment is defined even for a nonintegrable\nintegrand, where the integral is set to zero. Every theorem that reads this\nexpression as a moment therefore assumes integrability.\n\nAgainst a nonnegative weight, the moment cannot exceed the largest value of\nthe field times the mass of the weight.\n\nIf  is almost everywhere nonnegative and integrable, and\n is integrable, thenBecause  takes values in the extended nonnegative reals, this estimate\nalso covers unbounded slices without an auxiliary boundedness hypothesis. The\nweight need not have mass one.\n\nFirst bound the absolute value of the Bochner integral by the integral of the\nabsolute value and rewrite that integral as a nonnegative extended integral.\nAlmost everywhere, nonnegativity of  and the definition of  give\n\nMonotonicity of the nonnegative Lebesgue integral permits this pointwise bound, after which the\nconstant  is pulled outside. Integrability and nonnegativity of \nidentify the remaining extended integral with\n.\n\nDividing the estimate by a positive mass turns it into a lower bound for\n in terms of the moment, so once a PDE argument has driven\n to infinity the supremum must follow.\n\nIf the nonnegative integrable weight has positive mass, the weighted field is\nintegrable eventually, and  as\n, then the extended pointwise spatial supremum tends to\n.The theorem works along any filter of times and needs no spatial slice\nbound. A PDE-specific theorem supplies divergence of the chosen moment;\neventual finiteness of the slices is the additional condition needed to\nconclude divergence of the spatial supremum norm.\n\nDivide the weighted-moment estimate by the positive finite mass\n. The resulting quantity\n tends to  and is\neventually bounded above by . The order characterization of\nconvergence to the top element therefore proves divergence of the extended\nspatial supremum.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity\u0009Finite-Time Singularity Interfaces","header":"6.7.1.5.3. Weighted-moment bridge","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces--Weighted-moment-bridge"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Fourier-and-Laplace-Representations--Reconstructing-heat-and-waves":{"contents":"For evolution equations, the multiplier depends on time. Heat damps high\nfrequencies by ; waves rotate the displacement and\nvelocity modes by cosine and sine. The figure fixes the initial displacement\nof each mode at one and its initial velocity at zero. The higher frequency\ndecays faster under heat flow and oscillates faster under the wave equation.\nThese are the same spatial modes, with two different time evolutions.\n\n\n\nTo reconstruct a classical solution, we also have to justify the derivatives\nof the inverse integral and recover the initial data.\n\nLet  and let  be bounded,\ncontinuous, and integrable. For , the inverse Fourier formulais real, jointly smooth, and satisfies . As\n from positive times, .\nThe evolved Fourier data are integrable at positive time even when\n.If  as well, the same formula at  equals\n pointwise and satisfies the full pointwise heat Cauchy predicate.\nReal Schwartz data satisfy all these hypotheses.\n\nThe Fourier transform of an integrable datum is bounded. Multiplication by\nthe positive-time Gaussian therefore produces an integrable frequency\nprofile. Fourier pairing and the Gaussian transform identify its inverse as\n. The proved heat-kernel construction then supplies\njoint smoothness, the analytic heat equation, and the joint initial trace.\nAt exactly zero time the multiplier is one, so ordinary Fourier inversion\nuses the additional integrability of . Schwartz decay supplies\nthat assumption in the final corollary.\n\nLet the real displacement  and velocity\n be integrable, with both Fourier transforms\nintegrable. Thenis real and jointly  at every real time, and satisfies\n, , and .\nThe field and its actual time derivative also have the corresponding joint\nspace-time initial traces. Here \nwith value one at zero, so the zero-frequency velocity multiplier is .\nReal Schwartz data give the stated Cauchy solution without extra\nintegrability assumptions.\n\nFourier inversion sends the cosine mode to\n. Express the sinc mode as an integral of phases\nover ; integrability justifies Fubini, and inversion gives\n. Thus the full formula, including\nthe velocity contribution, equals d'Alembert's solution. Its established\nregularity, wave equation, assigned data, and joint derivative trace\ntransfer through this identity.\n\nIn higher dimensions, replace  by the Euclidean norm\n. Each frequency still solves the same scalar oscillator\nequation. The extra analytic work is in putting the frequencies back\ntogether: two spatial derivatives multiply the integrand by two frequency\nfactors, and those factors must remain integrable. Schwartz data give us\nexactly this room to differentiate.\n\nLet . Set\n and defineThen  is jointly  on all of  andBoth initial values are also joint limits as .\nThe real part in the definition gives a real field directly. The formula\nincludes , where the velocity multiplier is , and every\nfinite spatial dimension, including .\n\nThe cosine and sinc modes solve . On each bounded\ntime interval, their values and first two time derivatives are bounded by\npolynomials in  times .\nMathlib's Schwartz Fourier transform supplies integrable bounds for all\nthese moments.Apply the parameter integral theorem twice, the second time to an integral\nwith values in continuous linear maps. This gives the joint Hessian.\nIts time-time entry and the sum of its spatial diagonal entries agree\nbecause . Taking real parts preserves that\nidentity. At time zero, the cosine multiplier is one and the sinc\nmultiplier vanishes; their time derivatives are zero and one.\nFourier inversion therefore recovers  and , and joint continuity\nof the field and its first derivative gives the two traces.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Fourier and Laplace Representations","header":"9.2.1.7.2. Reconstructing heat and waves","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Fourier-and-Laplace-Representations--Reconstructing-heat-and-waves"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives","header":"4.6. Coverage and Open Problems","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature--Panel-rules-of-any-order":{"contents":"For a smooth integrand the practical way to reduce error is to cut the\ninterval into  equal panels and apply a low-order rule on each. The only\ninformation the composite construction needs about the local rule is a\none-panel bound of the form\n\n\n\nSumming  such bounds over panels of length  gives\n. The library records the pair (rule, ) with the\nproof of the one-panel bound as a PanelRule m, and builds composite\ncertificates, tolerance planning, and adaptive bisection once for every\norder .\n\nA panel rule of order  is a normalized rule  with a positive\nconstant  and a proof that, on every ordered interval , every\n with  there satisfies\n. Its composite certificate on  equal panels, given a\npanelwise derivative bounder with common bound , has error budget\n. Simpson's rule and the two-point Gauss–Legendre rule\nare panel rules of order four.\n\nOn an ordered interval, for  with\n there,Both rules are exact through degree one, and they are panel rules of order\ntwo with  and .\n\nTwo integrations by parts write each error as .\nFor the trapezoid rule the kernel is , of mass\n. For the midpoint rule it is  on the left half and\n on the right, of mass . Bound  by .\n\n\n\nThe figure measures the composite error for  against the\npanel count on logarithmic axes. On the left are the two second-order rules,\non the right Simpson's rule and the two-point Gauss rule. Each measured error\nsits below its dashed proved bound , and each curve has slope\n, so halving the panel width divides the error by  or by . The\nconstants fall in the order the denominators , , , \npredict: at every panel count the midpoint error is about half the trapezoid\nerror, and the Gauss error about two thirds of Simpson's.\n\nBecause the budget is an explicit function of , the number of panels\nneeded for a tolerance  can be computed in advance. The planner\nnever evaluates the integrand; it needs only the derivative bound .\n\nLet , , ,  and , put\n, and let\n. Then\n. Consequently, for a panel rule of\norder , an integrand in , , and a derivative bounder whose\nbound on every subinterval is at most , the composite certificate on the\nplanned mesh has error budget at most , and its value is within\n of .\n\nThe ceiling gives , and raising both sides to the\npower  gives . The composite budget is\n, and certificate soundness bounds the actual error by the budget.\n\nFor  we use the classical one-panel constants for Simpson's rule and the\ntwo-point Gauss rule. We prove their composite bounds and choose meshes with\nplanners in exact rational arithmetic that run inside Lean.\n\nFor an ordered interval , a  scalar integrand, and a bound \non its fourth within-derivative, the Peano-kernel argument gives the classical\none-panel Simpson estimate\n, returned as a\nQuadratureCert.\n\nRepresent the Simpson error as the integral of the fourth derivative against\nits Peano kernel, whose two polynomial pieces live on  and\n; four integrations by parts produce it, and cubic exactness\ncancels all boundary polynomial terms. The kernel has fixed sign and exact\nmass . Bounding the fourth derivative by  gives the stated\nnorm estimate.\n\nFor an ordered interval , a  scalar integrand, and a bound \non its fourth within-derivative, the two-point Gauss–Legendre Peano kernel is\nnonnegative and has mass . Consequently\n, with the irrational\nnodes treated exactly; the estimate is returned as a QuadratureCert.\n\nUse cubic exactness to express the rule error through the fourth-order Peano\nkernel. The exact two Gauss nodes make the boundary terms vanish, and the\nkernel is nonnegative with mass . The uniform fourth-derivative\nbound therefore yields the claimed certificate.\n\nAny local quadrature constructor with certified error\n can be accumulated over  equal panels. The generic theorem\nproduces error  and a canonical flattened ordered term list\nwhose exact weighted sum equals the composite value. Smoothness is assumed\nonly on the root interval, and one IteratedDerivativeBounderOn supplies each\npanel's within-derivative bound together with a common upper bound .\nAnalytic composition and finite-precision execution therefore share one\nrule-independent boundary.\n\nSplit the interval into  equal panels and add the local integral\nidentities. The triangle inequality sums the local errors. Since each panel\nhas length , the sum of  terms\n simplifies to . Flattening the panel\nterm lists preserves the same finite weighted sum.\n\nFor  equal Simpson panels, an integrand that is  on ,\nand certified panelwise fourth within-derivative bounds at most , summing\nthe sharp local certificates gives\n.\nEach panel consists of the usual two half-subintervals.\n\nInstantiate the generic composite theorem with the sharp one-panel Simpson\ncertificate and denominator . The common fourth-derivative bound\ndominates every panel certificate, and the generic error calculation gives\nthe displayed  estimate.\n\nFor  equal two-point Gauss–Legendre panels and the same root-local\nsmoothness and panelwise derivative-bound hypotheses, the composite certificate has error\n. Its ordered term list retains the two exact irrational\nnodes on every panel and is proved equal to the analytic composite value.\n\nInstantiate the uniform composite construction with the sharp two-point\nGauss–Legendre certificate and denominator . Summing the panel\ncertificates gives the global error, while the generic flattening theorem\nretains the exact ordered nodes and weights.\n\nThe composite rule has a canonical flattened list of ordered weighted terms.\nIts exact-real weighted sum is proved equal to the analytic composite Simpson\nvalue. Verified rational, interval, or floating-point evaluators can therefore\nevaluate the same term list.For the canonical panel-major list ,The canonical term list fixes the nodes and accumulation order; this equality\ncertifies its exact-real value. Equality of sums alone would not fix an order.\n\nUnfold the uniform composite rule panel by panel. On each panel the three\nlisted weighted terms are definitionally the transported Simpson value.\nInduction over the panel list shows that flattening and then taking the\nweighted sum equals the sum of the analytic panel values.\n\nEvery normalized exact-rational rule compiles uniformly to N > 0 panels by\nmapping a source term  on panel  to\n. The cast rule is proved equal term-for-term to the\ncanonical analytic uniform-composite list. This preserves both the exact-real\nvalue and the panel-major accumulation schedule used by verified numerical\nbackends.\n\nMap every rational source node and weight by the affine panel formula and cast\nthe result to the reals. Field identities show term by term that the cast node\nand weight equal those of the analytic transported rule. Mapping this equality\nover panels and flattening proves equality of both schedules and values.\n\nLet . For a positive tolerance, choosing\n proves\n. This is the case  of the planner for an\nerror model  of any order , and it is the rule-independent\nplanner behind every fourth-order uniform composite rule.\n\nIf  the bound holds for every . Otherwise the ceiling construction gives\n and . Positivity permits raising the first\ninequality to the fourth power, yielding . Rearranging with\n proves .\n\nFor exact rational error data, the executable planner rounds\n upward and takes one more than the integer fourth root\nof the result (Mathlib's Nat.nthRoot 4). The resulting positive natural panel\ncount satisfies the same fourth-order inequality without evaluating a\nnoncomputable real root.\n\nMathlib's Nat.lt_pow_nthRoot_add_one says that one more than the integer\nfourth root of  has fourth power above it, so the chosen\nnatural's fourth power is at least the exact rational ratio. Rearranging with\n gives the same tolerance estimate as the real planner.\n\nSubstituting the sharp Simpson denominator  into the executable\nrational planner produces a panel count whose composite error bound\n is at most the requested rational tolerance.\n\nApply the rational fourth-order planner with denominator , the error\nconstant  of the composite Simpson theorem: its panel count\n satisfies  in exact rational arithmetic.\n\nSubstituting the two-point Gauss–Legendre denominator  gives the\ncorresponding executable panel count, whose composite error bound\n is at most the requested rational tolerance.\n\nApply the same executable fourth-order planner with denominator , the\nerror constant of the composite two-point Gauss–Legendre theorem: its panel\ncount  satisfies  in exact rational arithmetic.\n\nGiven root-interval smoothness and certified panelwise fourth-derivative\nbounds, the planned Simpson and two-point Gauss–Legendre constructors return\nordinary QuadratureCerts whose semantic error is at most the user's positive\ntolerance. The public conclusion refers only to the integral, computed rule\nvalue, and tolerance; panel selection remains an internal construction.For either planned fourth-order rule ,\n\nThe planner chooses a positive panel count whose formula is below\n. Build the corresponding composite Simpson or Gauss–Legendre\ncertificate from the supplied smoothness and derivative bounds, then compose\nits analytic error estimate with the planner inequality.\n\nMathlib's composite trapezoidal rule is exposed through the common certificate\ninterface. Assume  is  on  and\n for every real , where  denotes the\nsecond iterated within-derivative. For  subintervals the error is at most\n.\n\nUnder these same regularity and derivative-bound hypotheses, for a positive\ntolerance the checked planner chooses\n with\n. The bound forces , and the resulting composite trapezoidal\ncertificate has error at most the requested tolerance. The square-root choice\nretains the standard  mesh scaling.\n\nThe ceiling square-root choice ensures  and .\nRearranging gives ; substituting the trapezoidal error\nconstant  into the composite trapezoidal certificate\nproves the result.\n\nA QuadratureTree records a rule-independent binary subdivision. Each leaf\ncontains an ordinary local quadrature certificate and integrability evidence;\nadjacent children compose by interval-integral additivity. The root therefore\ncertifies the sum of all leaf values with the sum of their error budgets. An\nadaptive tactic or external checker may choose the tree; soundness does not\ndepend on that choice.If  is the set of leaves, then the root certificate states\n\nInduct on the binary tree. A leaf is exactly its stored local certificate. At\na branch, interval-integral additivity splits the root integral across the two\nadjacent children; apply the two inductive estimates and the triangle\ninequality. Their values and budgets add, giving the recursively stored root\ncertificate.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Certified Integration\u0009Certified Quadrature","header":"10.8.1.5. Panel rules of any order","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature--Panel-rules-of-any-order"}});